#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180684 :tutorial/content "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an odd exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^3 = 125\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x$.\n\nSince $n=3$ is odd, there will be only one real solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} &= 125 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{x^{3}} &= \\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\\nx &= \\sqrt[3]{5^{3}} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5$.\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n> *When $n$ is odd, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$, we get only one solution:* $x = \\sqrt[n]{a}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3deb3f5-8f54-5126-a222-4b9346b3c89f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
